WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.640
OK, so now we're going to focus on how to optimize joining tables in SQL.

2
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.920
So the first tip here is to understand the speed of joins and to use inner join when it's possible.

3
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.440
Well, as we learned before, we have different types of joins.

4
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.520
We have the inner, left, right, and outer join.

5
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.040
And if you talk about the performance, the best performance you will get from that inner join.

6
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.360
And that's because SQL can work only on the matching rows.

7
00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.959
That means the effort and the processing time is better than the other joins.

8
00:00:32.959 --> 00:00:36.639
Now in the next one in ranking, we have the left and right joins.

9
00:00:36.639 --> 00:00:42.000
They are slightly slower than the inner join because usually they process more data and

10
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.959
more rows than the inner join because SQL will work not only with the matching rows

11
00:00:46.959 --> 00:00:49.099
as well with the unmatching rows.

12
00:00:49.099 --> 00:00:53.139
So for right and left, SQL has to do more stuff than the inner join.

13
00:00:53.139 --> 00:00:56.959
And now the worst type of joins, we have the outer join.

14
00:00:56.959 --> 00:01:02.720
And that's because this type works with the biggest number of rows compared to the other types.

15
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.400
It's going to present unmatching rows from the left and from the right tables.

16
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.980
So that means SQL has a lot to do, and that's why this join has the worst performance.

17
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:18.160
So here my advice is always try to use the inner join if it's enough to work with the matching rows.

18
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.360
And if the matching rows is not enough, then go with the left join maybe.

19
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.180
But try your best always to bring the inner join instead of left join.

20
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.540
But don't forget inner join filters the data.

21
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:35.139
Okay, the next one it says, use explicit join, the ANSI join, instead of implicit join.

22
00:01:35.139 --> 00:01:37.959
Well, it is considered as a bad practice.

23
00:01:37.959 --> 00:01:42.500
If you join tables like this, the implicit join or the non-ANSI join, it's better to

24
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.360
use the normal modern join where you use the inner join, for example.

25
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.220
About the performance, there is like no differences between them.

26
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.019
And for this scenario, it's very simple.

27
00:01:52.019 --> 00:01:57.300
But if you have like a complex query, then joining tables like this might be very confusing,

28
00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.599
really hard to read, and as well complex to optimize.

29
00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:04.519
That's why the best practice says, go with the normal inner join.

30
00:02:04.519 --> 00:02:08.419
So go with the ANSI join instead of the non-ANSI join.

31
00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:13.600
Okay, to the next tip, make sure to index the columns used in their own clause.

32
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.380
So we have to go and make sure that both of those columns has an index, because indexes

33
00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.919
speed up the lookup process.

34
00:02:20.919 --> 00:02:24.820
Without an index, the SQL might go and do a full table scan.

35
00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:29.940
Without an index on those columns, the database might go and scan the entire tables in order

36
00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.820
to find the match, and that is really slow if you have big tables.

37
00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:38.559
So now if you go to the customers over here, and then to the indexes, we can see that we

38
00:02:38.559 --> 00:02:42.639
have an index, a clustered index for the customer ID.

39
00:02:42.639 --> 00:02:47.320
But if you check the customer ID in the orders, we don't have an index for that.

40
00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.520
So this one doesn't have an index.

41
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.720
So in order to fix that, we're going to go and create a non-clustered index on the table

42
00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:57.479
orders for the customers ID, since it is a foreign key.

43
00:02:57.479 --> 00:03:01.660
So once we do that, we have now an index for both of those columns.

44
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.039
And with that, our join is going to be faster.

45
00:03:04.039 --> 00:03:09.199
Okay, so now we come to a tip where we say, really, it depends on, there is like not one

46
00:03:09.199 --> 00:03:11.039
clear way on how to do it.

47
00:03:11.039 --> 00:03:15.539
But let's say if you have a big tables, it is better to filter data before joining.

48
00:03:15.539 --> 00:03:19.779
And here we have like three different scenarios that's going to deliver the same results.

49
00:03:19.779 --> 00:03:22.820
But of course, the question is, which one is the best for performance.

50
00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.300
So now let's have a look to them.

51
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.059
What we are doing here, we are just joining two tables, and then we are filtering the

52
00:03:28.059 --> 00:03:32.179
result based on the order status that comes from the orders.

53
00:03:32.179 --> 00:03:35.740
So in the first query, what we are doing, we are first joining tables.

54
00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:39.240
And at the end, we are using WHERE clause in order to filter the data.

55
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:43.460
So by looking to this, we are just filtering the data after joining the tables.

56
00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:45.339
But there is another way on how to do it.

57
00:03:45.339 --> 00:03:51.100
You can go and join the tables, but on the join condition, you can go and add this order

58
00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.020
status equals to delivered.

59
00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.460
So we are matching the data by the customer ID.

60
00:03:55.460 --> 00:04:01.460
And at the same time, we are filtering the data by the order status since we are using the inner join.

61
00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.119
So the filtering is happening during the join.

62
00:04:04.119 --> 00:04:07.460
Or you can do it like this, where we have here more stuff to be added.

63
00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.419
Where we don't join the table directly with the orders, we first prepare the table orders

64
00:04:12.419 --> 00:04:15.119
before joining it with the customers.

65
00:04:15.119 --> 00:04:18.940
And here our preparation is, we are just selecting the columns that we need.

66
00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:24.119
And we are already filtering the data before doing the join using the subquery.

67
00:04:24.119 --> 00:04:27.700
But if you run all those queries, you will get the exact same results.

68
00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.679
And of course, there is another way on how to do it.

69
00:04:29.679 --> 00:04:32.980
You can go and prepare the data, not in subquery.

70
00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:38.220
You can go and use a CTE, and then join the result of the CTE with the table customers.

71
00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:43.359
So now about the performance, if your query is like small, not that complex, and as well,

72
00:04:43.359 --> 00:04:49.760
you don't have a big data inside your tables, all those three queries can deliver the same performance.

73
00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.519
I know it might sound weird because here we are like filtering after joining, or here

74
00:04:54.519 --> 00:04:57.160
we are filtering during the join.

75
00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:01.679
Normally in databases, the SQL optimizers are now very smart, can understand that there

76
00:05:01.679 --> 00:05:06.480
is a filter here, and decide on the best execution plan for you.

77
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.600
So actually, wherever you put your filter after, during, or before, the SQL is smart

78
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.279
enough to do it correctly.

79
00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:17.320
So if you don't have a complex query, and you don't have like big tables, go with the

80
00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.320
one that suits you.

81
00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.399
And I really recommend you to go with the first one because it's logical and easier to understand.

82
00:05:23.399 --> 00:05:29.040
But if you have big tables and complex queries, the best practice says, try always to prepare

83
00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.160
the data before joining it.

84
00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:37.440
So try to isolate and abstract the pre-step in a subquery or in a CTE before joining it

85
00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.160
with any other tables.

86
00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:43.000
And in many scenarios in my project where I have a big table, this did help, where the

87
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.839
execution plan was better if I isolate and prepare the data before joining it.

88
00:05:47.839 --> 00:05:52.600
So if you have small or medium tables, go with the normal way, use the WHERE clause.

89
00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:57.519
But if you have complex big tables, prepare the data in a subquery or a CTE, and then

90
00:05:57.519 --> 00:05:59.519
join it with the tables.

91
00:05:59.519 --> 00:06:04.359
Okay, now moving on to tip number 12, it is similar to the previous one, but this time

92
00:06:04.359 --> 00:06:07.899
it says aggregate data before joining tables.

93
00:06:07.899 --> 00:06:11.820
And again, it is a special case to improve the performance of big tables.

94
00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:16.220
So now we have the following scenario where we are joining the orders and the customers,

95
00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:21.019
and we are aggregating the data by the customer ID, but we are just joining the table customers

96
00:06:21.019 --> 00:06:22.880
because we need the first name.

97
00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.359
So as a result, we have the customer ID, the first name and the order count.

98
00:06:27.359 --> 00:06:32.880
So the standard way is to join the tables and then do a group by in order to summarize the data.

99
00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:37.799
Now, if you look to this query, we actually don't need the join in order to do the aggregations.

100
00:06:37.799 --> 00:06:43.579
We can do first the aggregation, like preparing the orders with the aggregated data, and then

101
00:06:43.579 --> 00:06:48.160
join the result with the customers in order to get the first name.

102
00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.500
So again, we prepare first, and then we do the join.

103
00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:55.700
And we can do that using either the subqueries or using the CTE.

104
00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:59.619
So in this scenario, first we are doing the group by, we are aggregating the data.

105
00:06:59.619 --> 00:07:04.700
And the result of this is joined with the customers tables in order to get the first name.

106
00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:08.660
Now, there are like many ways on how to do it, like for example, as well using the correlated

107
00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:13.739
queries, where we can go and use the subquery in the select statements, and then use the

108
00:07:13.739 --> 00:07:16.779
where condition over here to make the correlated query.

109
00:07:16.779 --> 00:07:21.980
Now, although three can deliver the same results, but the question here again, which one has

110
00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:22.980
the best performance?

111
00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:28.980
Well, I can go immediately and tell you that correlated subqueries are the worst one.

112
00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:33.619
Always avoid using correlated subqueries, they have really bad performance.

113
00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:39.619
And that's because SQL is going to go and do the aggregations for each customer individually.

114
00:07:39.619 --> 00:07:44.299
So it's going to go like for each row and doing aggregation, then to the next row and so on.

115
00:07:44.299 --> 00:07:45.299
So it takes a long time.

116
00:07:45.299 --> 00:07:48.339
So this is bad practices, don't use it.

117
00:07:48.339 --> 00:07:51.959
Now we are left again with the first option and the second option.

118
00:07:51.959 --> 00:07:55.820
And here my tip going to be like the previous one, I'm going to say if you have small to

119
00:07:55.820 --> 00:08:02.019
medium size of tables, then go with this one because it is easier to read and understand.

120
00:08:02.019 --> 00:08:05.940
And then you will gain exactly the same performance as this subquery.

121
00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:11.299
But if your tables are big, the best practices is to prepare first the data to go above the

122
00:08:11.299 --> 00:08:17.739
data to filter the data and to isolate it in a subquery or a CTE before joining it with

123
00:08:17.739 --> 00:08:19.880
the final table in the final query.

124
00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:24.820
But again, here only for big tables and always test, check the execution plan, whether you

125
00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:26.739
are really getting any benefits from it.

126
00:08:26.739 --> 00:08:32.299
All right, so if you have big tables, try to prepare the data first in CTE subquery and then join.

127
00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:38.380
Okay, moving on to the next tip, it says use union instead of OR operator in joins.

128
00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:43.380
So what this means, sometimes let's say that you are joining two tables, the customers and the orders.

129
00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:48.299
And now about the join key, you can see over here, it says the customer ID should be equal

130
00:08:48.299 --> 00:08:54.500
to the customer ID from the orders or the customer ID should be equal to the salesperson's ID.

131
00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:58.200
If one of these two conditions is fulfilled, then we have a match.

132
00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:02.380
And I can tell you the OR operator over here is a performance killer.

133
00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:05.239
It has really bad performance, so try to avoid it.

134
00:09:05.239 --> 00:09:07.559
Don't use OR in the joins.

135
00:09:07.559 --> 00:09:13.320
It has a lot of problems like it avoid indexes, it create like loop joins, and so on.

136
00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:16.380
That's why we consider it as a bad practice.

137
00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:21.099
And now in order to get the same results, we can go and split the joins.

138
00:09:21.099 --> 00:09:22.900
So we can go and have two queries.

139
00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:28.140
The first query is joining the data based on the customer ID and the second query based

140
00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:29.900
on the salesperson.

141
00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:33.239
And then we go and merge those two results using the union.

142
00:09:33.239 --> 00:09:39.099
It sounds like bigger and too much for the SQL, but with this, you will get better performance

143
00:09:39.099 --> 00:09:42.299
than using this simple OR operator.

144
00:09:42.299 --> 00:09:47.900
So again, if you have big tables, try to avoid using OR and instead of that, go and use union.

145
00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:52.780
Okay, the next tip says, check for nested loops and use SQL hints.

146
00:09:52.859 --> 00:09:56.479
Now imagine that we have like big tables and we are joining tables.

147
00:09:56.479 --> 00:10:01.539
So now if you are checking the execution plan, you have to check always the join type.

148
00:10:01.539 --> 00:10:05.900
So for example, here it is using the nested loops, which is of course, it's okay because

149
00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:07.419
we have small tables.

150
00:10:07.419 --> 00:10:12.659
But if you have big tables and still SQL is using for some reason, the nested loops, then

151
00:10:12.659 --> 00:10:14.200
this is alerting.

152
00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:18.619
So in order to change this, what we can do, we can go and use the SQL hints in order to

153
00:10:18.619 --> 00:10:21.260
force SQL to use the hash join.

154
00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:25.659
Hash join is really good if you have a big table, like for example, the orders that is

155
00:10:25.659 --> 00:10:28.460
joins with a small table like the customers.

156
00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:32.599
So now what we can do at the end, we can write over here, option hash join.

157
00:10:32.599 --> 00:10:36.320
So let's go and execute it and let's check the execution plan.

158
00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.580
And with that, we have forced SQL to use the hash join or hash match.

159
00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:43.580
Again, you have here ready to evaluate your tables.

160
00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:48.099
If you have like small tables, don't bother with that, but if you have big tables and

161
00:10:48.099 --> 00:10:52.619
SQL is still doing the nested loops, nested loops are usually very slow because you have

162
00:10:52.619 --> 00:10:54.659
a lot of iterations and so on.

163
00:10:54.659 --> 00:10:58.580
And with the hash join, that small table is going to be stored in the memory.

164
00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:02.219
And then you have really a quick matching between the two tables.

165
00:11:02.219 --> 00:11:06.900
So those are all the best practice and tips on how to optimize joining tables in SQL.

166
00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:10.419
All right, so now we're going to talk about union and here is the best practices.

167
00:11:10.419 --> 00:11:16.119
It says use union all instead of using union if duplicates are acceptable.

168
00:11:16.119 --> 00:11:17.119
So it's very simple.

169
00:11:17.119 --> 00:11:21.880
If the duplicates are acceptable, or let's say that there is no duplicates, then don't

170
00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:25.520
go with the union because it needs more time to be executed.

171
00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:29.840
SQL has to go and check row by row whether we have duplicates or not.

172
00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:33.780
And this usually takes longer time than using the union all.

173
00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:39.039
So if duplicates are acceptable or you don't have any duplicates in your data, go with the union all.

174
00:11:39.039 --> 00:11:43.359
SQL just have to go and merge all the data without checking anything and the performance

175
00:11:43.359 --> 00:11:44.359
is going to be faster.

176
00:11:44.479 --> 00:11:46.919
All right, the next one is a little bit tricky.

177
00:11:46.919 --> 00:11:53.000
So it says use union all together with the distinct instead of using union if the duplicates

178
00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.099
are not acceptable.

179
00:11:54.099 --> 00:11:56.080
So you want to remove the duplicates.

180
00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:59.479
So we have learned that in order to do that, we're going to go and use the union, it's

181
00:11:59.479 --> 00:12:03.719
going to go and merge the data and as well remove the duplicates, which is really okay

182
00:12:03.719 --> 00:12:07.080
to use it if you have like smaller data or medium.

183
00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:10.719
But let's say that you have like millions of rows, which is really okay if you have

184
00:12:10.719 --> 00:12:12.760
like medium and small tables.

185
00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:17.119
But again, here, if you have huge tables, big tables, hundreds of millions, the best

186
00:12:17.119 --> 00:12:23.080
practice says go with the union all and afterwards use a distinct.

187
00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:27.460
So in the sub query, we are using union all, but in order to remove the duplicates, we

188
00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:28.960
use the distinct.

189
00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:32.239
But again, here you have to test it to check the execution plan.

190
00:12:32.239 --> 00:12:35.280
If you are getting benefit, then go with this version.

191
00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:38.260
But if your data is not really big, you have hundreds of thousands.

192
00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:42.679
So go with just with the normal union, the code is smaller and you will get the same

193
00:12:42.799 --> 00:12:46.799
effects, but only for large tables, you can go with this best practice.

194
00:12:46.799 --> 00:12:48.960
So that's all what I have for you for the union.

